    {
      "title": "Experts warn AI copyright changes could breach Australian constitution and spark huge compensation rights case",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/oct/31/experts-warn-ai-copyright-changes-could-breach-australian-constitution-and-spark-huge-compensation-rights-case",
      "description": "<p>Scraping copyrighted material from internet could ‘potentially’ amount to acquisition of property by government, Anne Twomey says</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Altering copyright law to allow AI companies to scrape music, books and journalism could trigger a constitutional breach, Coalition senators and the recording industry have warned, as they raise new fears it could open Australia to massive legal action and compensation claims from rights holders worldwide.</p><p>The attorney general’s department admitted it was “extremely alive” to the issue, and Australia’s leading constitutional expert, Anne Twomey, agreed there was “potentially” a concern – depending how legislation was drafted – that granting AI companies permission to scrape copyrighted material from the internet could amount to an acquisition of property by the government.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/oct/31/experts-warn-ai-copyright-changes-could-breach-australian-constitution-and-spark-huge-compensation-rights-case\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-30T14:00:18"
    },
    {
      "title": "Feral deer will become Australia’s next feral pig problem without prompt action, report warns",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/30/australia-feral-deer-problem-report-national-action-plan",
      "description": "<p>The invasive species has increased in spread and numbers and is costing farmers millions of dollars</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>An unpublished government report on feral deer shows Australia is failing in its efforts to stop the spread of what one advocate warns could become “Australia’s next <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/nov/30/feral-pigs-queensland-rainforests-rivers-sacred-sites\">feral pig</a>”.</p><p>The five years from 2020-2025 over which the national feral deer action plan was assessed covers a period in which the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/nov/27/deer-high-visibility-jacket-british-columbia\">famously elusive</a> species has increasingly encroached upon major cities, leading to <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2020/oct/15/oh-deer-animal-seen-crossing-anzac-bridge-and-near-train-tracks-in-central-sydney\">more frequent encounters</a> – including a memorable occasion in which two men in various degrees of undress were fined after becoming lost in the bush when they were <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2021/jun/28/buck-naked-nude-sunbathers-fleeing-deer-fined-for-breaking-sydney-lockdown\">spooked by deer while nude sunbathing</a>.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/30/australia-feral-deer-problem-report-national-action-plan\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-30T03:30:42"
    },
    {
      "title": "A working-class Alaskan Native takes on a ‘multimillionaire’ Republican for seat in Congress",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/30/bill-hill-alaska-congressional-race",
      "description": "<p>Independent candidate Bill Hill is threatening to unseat Trump ally Nick Begich with a message of affordability</p><p>As <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans\">Republicans</a> fight to maintain their narrow majority in <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-congress\">Congress</a>, they face a unique challenge in <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/alaska\">Alaska</a> – not from a Democrat, but from an independent commercial fisher and first-time candidate running to flip the state’s lone <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/house-of-representatives\">House</a> seat.</p><p>Bill Hill, who is an Alaska Native and has worked in education and construction, has positioned himself as a <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2026/sep/19/democrats-labor-union-congress-candidates-midterm-elections\">working-class</a> candidate who will fight to make life more affordable for people like himself and his family.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/30/bill-hill-alaska-congressional-race\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-30T16:00:17"
    }
  ]
